<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Ming</h1><p class="st-meta">1644 CE – 1662 CE · capital Nanjing, Fuzhou, Zhaoqing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The succession of Ming loyalist courts in southern China that resisted the Manchu conquest for eighteen years after the fall of Beijing.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shun-dynasty">Shun dynasty</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a> killed himself, officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> enthroned the Prince of Fu as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongguang-emperor">Hongguang Emperor</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> soon drove Li Zicheng out of Beijing and turned south.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Rival princes and factions undermined every Southern Ming court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> fell in 1645 after the massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longwu-emperor">Longwu Emperor</a>, enthroned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>, was captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> in 1646, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongli-emperor">Yongli Emperor</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhaoqing">Zhaoqing</a> and later the south-west. The Qing order to shave the forehead in 1645 drove many to resist, and former rebels such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-dingguo">Li Dingguo</a> and the coastal power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> cause.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in 1659, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongli-emperor">Yongli Emperor</a> took refuge with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a>, which handed him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> in 1662; he was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>. Loyalism lived on in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tungning">Kingdom of Tungning</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Taiwan</a> until 1683.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/southern-ming.jpg" alt="Southern Ming" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Ming.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1644 CE – 1662 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nanjing, Fuzhou, Zhaoqing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhu Yousong (Hongguang Emperor), Zhu Yujian (Longwu Emperor), Zhu Youlang (Yongli Emperor)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Confucianism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Southern Ming dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1653&amp;focus=southern-ming" aria-label="Southern Ming on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7736 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Ming lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8056 1267L7643 1242M8056 1267L7567 1068M8056 1267L8210 1581"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7643 1242h0M7567 1068h0M8210 1581h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8056 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nanjing. <a href="/atlas?year=1653&amp;focus=southern-ming">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
